WEBVTT

00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:04.810
Last updated: 29 July 20 26

00:00:05.746 --> 00:00:08.019
In plain terms.

00:00:08.791 --> 00:00:12.107
The documents, images, videos and files

00:00:12.173 --> 00:00:15.369
you make in Lolly stay on your device.

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:19.016
There are no accounts for ordinary use, no

00:00:19.088 --> 00:00:21.800
cookies from the app itself, and no

00:00:21.861 --> 00:00:24.173
analytics or trackers anywhere in the

00:00:24.234 --> 00:00:27.451
codebase - not "we don't use the data,"

00:00:27.513 --> 00:00:30.420
genuinely not present in the source.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.515
A short, complete list of exceptions

00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:36.150
exists where the software talks to a

00:00:36.209 --> 00:00:39.154
network at all, and every one of them is

00:00:39.214 --> 00:00:42.605
described below in specifics: what leaves,

00:00:42.681 --> 00:00:44.695
to whom, and when.

00:00:45.459 --> 00:00:48.282
The only exception that involves anything

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:51.229
personal is a sign-in you have to

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.244
explicitly start.

00:00:54.007 --> 00:00:56.988
If it isn't in this document, it doesn't

00:00:57.051 --> 00:00:58.144
happen.

00:00:59.286 --> 00:01:01.791
What this policy covers

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:13.525
Lolly is open-source software - an engine, several app shells «web, desktop, mobile, CLI», and a browser extension - that anyone can run.

00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:17.150
This policy has two parts:

00:01:17.882 --> 00:01:20.554
The software itself: what it does and

00:01:20.596 --> 00:01:23.424
doesn't do with your data, wherever it

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.643
runs.

00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:29.332
This is a property of the code, so it's

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.427
true of every Lolly deployment, ours or

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:34.194
anyone else's.

00:01:34.981 --> 00:01:37.833
lolly-tools, the reference deployment SUSE

00:01:37.912 --> 00:01:40.704
operates: the specific choices

00:01:41.225 --> 00:01:48.125
made running its optional server-side pieces «what's logged, for how long, by whom».

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:51.204
If you're using a self-hosted or

00:01:51.268 --> 00:01:54.083
enterprise Lolly instance, the software

00:01:54.143 --> 00:01:57.085
behaviour below still applies, but the

00:01:57.168 --> 00:02:00.169
operator of that instance - not SUSE - is

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:03.455
responsible for anything server-side:

00:02:03.521 --> 00:02:06.876
their render endpoint, their MCP server,

00:02:06.938 --> 00:02:09.088
their Content Credentials certificate

00:02:09.151 --> 00:02:11.869
authority, if they run one.

00:02:12.662 --> 00:02:15.793
Ask them for their own policy.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.370
See Adoption & Governance for what

00:02:19.446 --> 00:02:21.894
operating Lolly involves.

00:02:23.027 --> 00:02:26.055
The app: what stays on your device

00:02:27.059 --> 00:02:30.151
Lolly's web, desktop and mobile shells run

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:33.619
the entire render engine client-side.

00:02:34.384 --> 00:02:37.260
Opening a tool, filling in inputs,

00:02:37.329 --> 00:02:39.880
previewing and exporting all happen on

00:02:39.942 --> 00:02:43.236
your device - no server is involved, and

00:02:43.275 --> 00:02:46.269
the app works offline once loaded.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.817
The app sets no cookies.

00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:53.466
To function, it keeps a small amount of

00:02:53.528 --> 00:02:56.114
data on your device only, never

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:57.644
transmitted:

00:02:58.403 --> 00:03:01.156
Interface preferences - theme, language,

00:03:01.216 --> 00:03:04.104
sound settings, sidebar/zoom

00:03:05.013 --> 00:03:07.753
sizing, sort and view choices, which

00:03:07.843 --> 00:03:09.706
onboarding tips you've seen - in

00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:12.865
localStorage, so they're available before

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:15.295
the app has finished booting.

00:03:16.076 --> 00:03:19.133
An offline cache of the tool catalogue and

00:03:19.201 --> 00:03:22.021
asset previews, so the gallery

00:03:22.962 --> 00:03:25.693
works without a connection.

00:03:26.075 --> 00:03:32.650
Local usage counters for your profile card's stats «how many exports, which

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:40.575
tools» - a small bounded blob in localStorage, never read by us, never sent anywhere.

00:03:41.323 --> 00:03:43.653
Your own documents, saved sessions,

00:03:43.711 --> 00:03:46.183
uploaded assets and fonts - stored in

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:50.802
IndexedDB on your device, never uploaded,

00:03:50.867 --> 00:03:53.594
never read by anyone but you.

00:03:53.975 --> 00:03:59.750
None of this is shared, sold, or used to identify or track you.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:10.025
There is nothing to consent to, because there is no collection happening - only this notice, so you know what's kept and where.

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:20.525
Wipe all of it at any time with Profile → Clear all my data, or by clearing the site's storage in your browser.

00:04:20.875 --> 00:04:25.175
«Under the ePrivacy Directive Art.

00:04:25.525 --> 00:04:39.900
5«3», storage that is strictly necessary for the service you asked for doesn't require consent - only transparency, which is what this document and the in-app notice both are.»

00:04:40.658 --> 00:04:43.244
Your own backup of this data - the

00:04:43.328 --> 00:04:46.280
lolly-backup bundle produced by Export my

00:04:46.341 --> 00:04:49.881
data & render everything - is a file you

00:04:49.941 --> 00:04:51.795
keep and control.

00:04:52.546 --> 00:04:55.256
It never touches our servers unless you

00:04:55.318 --> 00:04:58.444
choose to send it somewhere yourself.

00:04:59.243 --> 00:05:01.793
See Data Transfer.

00:05:02.923 --> 00:05:05.439
On-device utilities

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:09.110
Some tools - Strip Hidden Data, Compress

00:05:09.197 --> 00:05:12.613
PDF, and others carrying the "Runs on your

00:05:12.672 --> 00:05:15.304
device" badge - operate on a file you

00:05:15.359 --> 00:05:16.595
provide.

00:05:17.366 --> 00:05:19.802
The file is read into memory in your

00:05:19.864 --> 00:05:23.016
browser, transformed locally, and offered

00:05:23.078 --> 00:05:25.244
back as a download.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.744
It is never uploaded, because there is no

00:05:28.864 --> 00:05:32.294
server in the path to upload it to.

00:05:33.068 --> 00:05:35.957
These utilities work offline, and their

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:39.386
output carries no watermark or metadata of

00:05:39.475 --> 00:05:42.433
ours - the point of most of them is to

00:05:42.493 --> 00:05:46.245
remove & protect data, not add risk.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:49.982
When the app talks to a network, in full

00:05:50.861 --> 00:05:53.793
The table below is the complete list of

00:05:53.889 --> 00:05:57.039
everything the app fetches or sends over a

00:05:57.099 --> 00:05:58.270
network.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:02.470
If it isn't here, the app doesn't do it.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:13.125
Every host in that table is also the complete allowlist in the app's Content-Security-Policy, which the browser enforces.

00:06:13.475 --> 00:06:29.800
So the list is not only a description of what the code does today, it is the boundary the browser holds the app to: a future change that tried to contact some other host would be blocked, not silently permitted.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:44.975
A deployment that wants none of the optional ones «an enterprise instance with its own fonts, say» removes those hosts from its policy and the features fail closed rather than reaching out.

00:06:45.325 --> 00:06:52.100
None of these send your documents, projects, sessions or uploaded files anywhere.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:07:04.675
They exist to bring things to your device «tools, fonts, models», never to send things from it, with the exceptions named explicitly in the sections below.

00:07:05.428 --> 00:07:08.718
A note on what we removed.

00:07:09.493 --> 00:07:12.873
Verify can check SEAL signatures, a scheme

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:15.732
where a file's signing key is published in

00:07:15.794 --> 00:07:17.119
DNS.

00:07:17.882 --> 00:07:21.426
Browsers can't make DNS queries, so any

00:07:21.488 --> 00:07:24.255
web implementation has to route the lookup

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:27.707
through a third-party DNS-over-HTTPS

00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:30.688
resolver - which would show that operator

00:07:30.749 --> 00:07:33.491
the domain being checked plus your IP

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:34.720
address.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:38.643
We used to use Cloudflare's.

00:07:39.409 --> 00:07:41.581
We don't any more, and there is no

00:07:41.638 --> 00:07:44.704
replacement: the web app now passes no

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:48.086
resolver at all, so SEAL verification here

00:07:48.146 --> 00:07:50.820
makes zero network requests.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.469
Files whose SEAL record carries its key

00:07:54.533 --> 00:07:58.268
inline still verify completely offline.

00:07:59.037 --> 00:08:02.318
Files whose key lives in DNS report "no

00:08:02.406 --> 00:08:05.441
key resolver" instead, and you can check

00:08:05.501 --> 00:08:08.732
those in the desktop or command-line app,

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.573
which resolve DNS natively through your

00:08:11.638 --> 00:08:15.170
own machine with no third party involved.

00:08:15.954 --> 00:08:18.895
You can confirm this yourself: greppable

00:08:18.958 --> 00:08:21.470
checks for this and every other claim on

00:08:21.511 --> 00:08:24.537
this page, with the exact commands and

00:08:24.593 --> 00:08:27.619
expected output, live at Verify It

00:08:27.668 --> 00:08:28.919
Yourself.

00:08:30.056 --> 00:08:32.428
Hot-linked render URL'S

00:08:33.399 --> 00:08:36.868
Currently switched off on lolly-tools.

00:08:37.653 --> 00:08:39.918
Every lolly-tools>.

00:08:40.723 --> 00:08:44.868
URL returns 404 today.

00:08:45.635 --> 00:08:47.996
The section below describes what the

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:51.485
feature does when an operator enables it,

00:08:51.544 --> 00:08:53.544
and why we have not.

00:08:54.295 --> 00:08:57.058
It will be turned on here once the service

00:08:57.133 --> 00:09:00.551
moves to SUSE-operated infrastructure, and

00:09:00.604 --> 00:09:03.795
this notice will change when it is.

00:09:04.568 --> 00:09:07.511
The app itself stays entirely on your

00:09:07.572 --> 00:09:08.794
device.

00:09:09.586 --> 00:09:12.120
Separately, an operator can enable

00:09:12.212 --> 00:09:15.819
hot-link render URL'S - /tool/ .

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:16.642
?

00:09:17.384 --> 00:09:20.224
- so a shared Lolly link can appear as a

00:09:20.284 --> 00:09:23.438
live image in a README, a wiki or a

00:09:23.498 --> 00:09:24.820
dashboard.

00:09:25.585 --> 00:09:28.241
Fetching one asks the server to render

00:09:28.301 --> 00:09:31.415
public tool and catalogue data with the

00:09:31.486 --> 00:09:34.395
inputs written into the URL.

00:09:35.189 --> 00:09:38.394
No accounts, no cookies, no state.

00:09:39.176 --> 00:09:42.720
The endpoint is anonymous, and nothing

00:09:43.614 --> 00:09:46.493
on your device is read.

00:09:47.272 --> 00:09:50.410
Your documents, sessions and uploads never

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.901
leave your browser - they cannot appear in

00:09:52.962 --> 00:09:54.795
these links at all.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.269
But the URL itself is recorded.

00:10:00.041 --> 00:10:02.798
A URL'S query string is part of the

00:10:02.857 --> 00:10:03.912
request

00:10:04.944 --> 00:10:07.346
line, so it lands in the hosting

00:10:07.408 --> 00:10:10.439
platform's ordinary access logs the same

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.444
way every requested path does.

00:10:14.199 --> 00:10:16.947
If a link's inputs contain someone's name

00:10:17.007 --> 00:10:20.157
or email - a name badge, an email

00:10:20.249 --> 00:10:23.503
signature - that text sits in those logs,

00:10:23.564 --> 00:10:26.290
and no amount of policy wording changes

00:10:26.352 --> 00:10:27.270
it.

00:10:28.043 --> 00:10:31.132
This is the specific reason the feature is

00:10:31.189 --> 00:10:33.469
off here rather than on.

00:10:34.244 --> 00:10:36.914
The inputs are public by construction

00:10:37.011 --> 00:10:39.859
anyway - they are whatever the link's

00:10:40.815 --> 00:10:44.095
author typed into the URL, readable by

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:46.594
anyone the link reaches.

00:10:47.367 --> 00:10:50.619
Don't put secrets in a shared link.

00:10:51.422 --> 00:10:54.703
Lolly offers link encryption for sensitive

00:10:54.766 --> 00:10:56.118
content.

00:10:56.897 --> 00:10:59.870
Responses are cached and rate-limited like

00:10:59.929 --> 00:11:02.798
any public image, and marked

00:11:03.813 --> 00:11:07.120
noindex so search engines don't index your

00:11:07.182 --> 00:11:08.394
renders.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:12.235
Self-hosting Lolly and don't want a public

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:13.969
render surface?

00:11:14.754 --> 00:11:18.072
Set LOLLY_DISABLE_RENDER_GET=1 - what

00:11:18.132 --> 00:11:21.335
lolly-tools itself currently does - and

00:11:21.431 --> 00:11:25.495
every one of these URL'S returns 404.

00:11:26.225 --> 00:11:31.175
The MCP server «optional, for AI agents»

00:11:31.525 --> 00:11:47.125
Lolly can also be reached by an AI agent over the Model Context Protocol - an operator-run endpoint «lolly-tools runs one; anyone can self-host their own, including fully air-gapped».

00:11:47.475 --> 00:11:55.875
It shares the render path's no-accounts posture, plus three tools that necessarily handle file bytes:

00:11:56.225 --> 00:12:02.600
lolly_transform «run an on-device utility server-side, on the calling

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:15.875
agent's behalf», lolly_verify «check Content Credentials» and lolly_redact «black out regions of an image or PDF» all accept a file's bytes from the caller.

00:12:16.225 --> 00:12:29.150
They are processed in-process, in memory, and the result is returned in that same call - the file is never written to disk and never stored once the request completes.

00:12:29.878 --> 00:12:31.867
Every other tool - lolly_render,

00:12:31.934 --> 00:12:35.119
lolly_build_url, lolly_list_tools,

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:48.275
lolly_describe_tool - works from parameters only «text, numbers, colours, URL'S, catalogue asset ids», the same inputs a hot-link render URL takes.

00:12:49.003 --> 00:12:51.436
Access is either a shared token the

00:12:51.498 --> 00:12:54.437
operator issues to clients they trust, or

00:12:55.454 --> 00:12:58.846
stateless OAuth 2 point 1: short-lived

00:12:58.934 --> 00:13:01.627
signed tokens verified against a shared

00:13:01.712 --> 00:13:05.012
secret, nothing stored server-side, and

00:13:05.043 --> 00:13:08.080
the token itself is never written to a log

00:13:08.147 --> 00:13:10.394
or a render URL.

00:13:11.125 --> 00:13:17.175
Content Credentials identity «a sign-in you have to start yourself»

00:13:17.525 --> 00:13:30.025
Lolly can seal a cryptographic Content Credential into your exports so anyone can verify, offline, that a file is unaltered since it left Lolly.

00:13:30.375 --> 00:13:45.500
That much is on by default and fully local - the signing key is generated on your device, is non-extractable «not even Lolly's own code can read it», and signing itself happens offline.

00:13:45.850 --> 00:14:00.500
This section covers the one optional step on top of that: enrolling a verified identity, so your exports say "Verified - signed by \ " instead of an anonymous key.

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:09.575
If you skip enrolment, nothing in this section applies to you, and no personal data ever leaves your device.

00:14:10.305 --> 00:14:13.259
If you do enrol, here is exactly what

00:14:13.321 --> 00:14:14.569
happens:

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:21.150
You choose a sign-in method - GitHub, Google, SUSE «Okta», or an emailed

00:14:21.922 --> 00:14:22.942
link.

00:14:23.729 --> 00:14:26.301
For the three OIDC providers, you're

00:14:26.364 --> 00:14:29.270
redirected to that provider's own login

00:14:29.334 --> 00:14:32.406
page, governed by their privacy policy,

00:14:32.464 --> 00:14:33.969
not ours.

00:14:34.762 --> 00:14:37.487
Lolly's certificate service receives back

00:14:37.583 --> 00:14:40.648
only a verified email address and the

00:14:40.709 --> 00:14:42.419
provider's name.

00:14:43.195 --> 00:14:45.690
For the email link, the address you type

00:14:45.732 --> 00:14:49.084
is passed to Resend, a transactional email

00:14:49.145 --> 00:14:52.820
API, solely to deliver that one link.

00:14:53.594 --> 00:14:56.062
A short-lived cookie protects the

00:14:56.129 --> 00:14:57.643
redirect.

00:14:58.432 --> 00:15:01.275
This is the one cookie the

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:05.853
entire Lolly system sets: lolly_ca_state,

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:10.492
HttpOnly, scoped to /api/ca,

00:15:10.556 --> 00:15:13.069
expiring within ten minutes.

00:15:13.816 --> 00:15:16.842
It carries a random value, not a tracking

00:15:16.902 --> 00:15:20.283
identifier, and exists only to stop the

00:15:20.368 --> 00:15:23.320
OAuth redirect being forged.

00:15:24.081 --> 00:15:26.694
It is cleared as soon as sign-in

00:15:26.757 --> 00:15:28.018
completes.

00:15:28.807 --> 00:15:31.947
Your IP address is used, briefly, to

00:15:32.008 --> 00:15:34.365
prevent abuse of the sign-in

00:15:34.975 --> 00:15:49.950
endpoints «so one script can't spam an inbox or exhaust the email quota» - held in server memory only, for a sliding window of about a minute, never written to a log or persisted anywhere.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:59.225
The certificate service issues a short-lived certificate «7, 30, 90 or 365

00:15:59.575 --> 00:16:11.250
days, your choice, capped by the operator's policy» binding your verified email to the public half of the keypair generated on your device.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.800
The private half never leaves your browser.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:20.318
Nothing about the issuance is recorded.

00:16:21.108 --> 00:16:24.167
The certificate service keeps no

00:16:25.099 --> 00:16:27.691
issuance log: not your email, not the

00:16:27.752 --> 00:16:30.322
provider, not a serial number, not a

00:16:30.383 --> 00:16:31.795
timestamp.

00:16:32.598 --> 00:16:36.368
No database, no log line, no webhook.

00:16:37.172 --> 00:16:40.463
Your email address exists in the request

00:16:40.585 --> 00:16:43.103
only long enough to be written into the

00:16:43.165 --> 00:16:46.669
certificate that your own device receives,

00:16:46.733 --> 00:16:49.307
and then it is gone from our side

00:16:49.376 --> 00:16:50.819
entirely.

00:16:51.586 --> 00:16:54.574
After that, signing is offline again for

00:16:54.634 --> 00:16:57.394
the certificate's whole lifetime.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.842
Exporting a file never contacts the

00:17:00.904 --> 00:17:04.794
certificate service - only enrolling did.

00:17:05.576 --> 00:17:08.544
The tradeoff, stated plainly.

00:17:09.317 --> 00:17:12.443
An earlier version of this service did log

00:17:12.521 --> 00:17:15.432
each issuance, so that a misissued or

00:17:15.506 --> 00:17:18.570
compromised certificate could be traced.

00:17:19.344 --> 00:17:22.259
We removed it, because that log was the

00:17:22.351 --> 00:17:25.179
only place in all of Lolly where personal

00:17:25.239 --> 00:17:29.044
data came to rest on a server, and we

00:17:29.099 --> 00:17:31.323
would rather not hold it than hold it

00:17:31.387 --> 00:17:32.620
carefully.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:35.417
What we give up is server-side

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:39.196
traceability: if a certificate is misused

00:17:39.261 --> 00:17:42.294
we cannot look up who obtained it.

00:17:43.068 --> 00:17:46.000
Certificates are short-lived by design - 7

00:17:46.088 --> 00:17:50.929
to 365 days, your choice, capped by the

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:54.200
operator - and expire on their own, which

00:17:54.262 --> 00:17:57.645
is the mitigation we rely on instead.

00:17:58.429 --> 00:18:01.474
Self-hosters whose own obligations require

00:18:01.575 --> 00:18:05.075
an audit log can add one, and become the

00:18:05.136 --> 00:18:08.495
controller of that data by doing so.

00:18:09.635 --> 00:18:12.014
The browser extension

00:18:12.941 --> 00:18:16.160
The Lolly URL Screenshot browser extension

00:18:16.233 --> 00:18:19.363
does not collect, store, or transmit any

00:18:19.424 --> 00:18:21.144
personal data.

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:24.695
No analytics, no tracking, no remote

00:18:24.786 --> 00:18:25.894
server.

00:18:26.682 --> 00:18:28.443
What it does.

00:18:29.213 --> 00:18:31.328
When you ask the Lolly web app to

00:18:31.408 --> 00:18:35.313
screenshot a URL, the extension opens that

00:18:35.397 --> 00:18:38.083
page in a temporary background tab,

00:18:38.159 --> 00:18:40.612
captures it in your browser using the

00:18:40.678 --> 00:18:44.111
DevTools Protocol, hands the image back to

00:18:44.171 --> 00:18:47.045
the app, and closes the tab.

00:18:47.809 --> 00:18:50.633
Everything happens locally, on your own

00:18:50.692 --> 00:18:52.744
device and network.

00:18:53.529 --> 00:18:54.784
Data.

00:18:55.586 --> 00:18:57.743
We collect nothing.

00:18:58.517 --> 00:19:01.693
The extension has no servers and makes no

00:19:01.756 --> 00:19:02.716
network

00:19:03.726 --> 00:19:06.243
requests of its own.

00:19:06.996 --> 00:19:09.664
Captured images go straight to the Lolly

00:19:09.737 --> 00:19:12.217
app in the same browser - never

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:16.267
uploaded by the extension.

00:19:17.039 --> 00:19:19.809
The URL'S you capture are used only to

00:19:19.871 --> 00:19:22.639
load that one page for that one

00:19:23.576 --> 00:19:25.041
screenshot.

00:19:25.826 --> 00:19:28.843
They are not logged or shared.

00:19:29.614 --> 00:19:31.168
Permissions.

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:37.750
debugger - to capture the rendered page via the DevTools Protocol «the

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:42.725
same mechanism the Lolly desktop app uses».

00:19:43.461 --> 00:19:46.579
tabs - to open and close the temporary tab

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:48.619
the page loads in.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:54.275
Host access « » - because the page you choose to capture can be

00:19:55.017 --> 00:19:56.919
on any site.

00:19:57.686 --> 00:20:01.281
Chrome surfaces this at install time as a

00:20:01.374 --> 00:20:03.519
broad permission warning.

00:20:04.304 --> 00:20:08.018
The extension only ever visits the URL you

00:20:08.082 --> 00:20:09.293
give it.

00:20:10.092 --> 00:20:13.158
None of these are used to read, monitor,

00:20:13.223 --> 00:20:16.077
or transmit your browsing beyond that one

00:20:16.137 --> 00:20:18.019
requested capture.

00:20:19.149 --> 00:20:21.344
Infrastructure logs

00:20:22.339 --> 00:20:24.720
Like any website, the servers behind

00:20:24.779 --> 00:20:27.333
lolly-tools - and behind any Lolly

00:20:27.393 --> 00:20:30.066
deployment - generate standard web-server

00:20:30.133 --> 00:20:32.911
access logs whenever a request reaches

00:20:32.946 --> 00:20:36.581
them at all: IP address, requested path,

00:20:36.651 --> 00:20:39.045
timestamp, user agent.

00:20:39.814 --> 00:20:42.676
That's baseline hosting behaviour, not

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:45.893
something Lolly adds on top, and it never

00:20:45.954 --> 00:20:48.886
contains the contents of your documents,

00:20:48.945 --> 00:20:51.511
because those never reach a server to

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:52.994
begin with.

00:20:53.759 --> 00:20:56.429
The one deliberate exception is a file you

00:20:56.487 --> 00:21:00.703
explicitly hand to an MCP lolly_transform,

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:03.875
lolly_verify or lolly_redact call, which

00:21:03.942 --> 00:21:06.458
is processed in memory and never written

00:21:06.546 --> 00:21:10.445
to disk or a log, as described above.

00:21:10.825 --> 00:21:15.400
Lolly's own code writes nothing to those logs.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:21.550
The MCP server contains no logging statements at all.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:41.025
The certificate service emits exactly two lines, both on failure and both deliberately stripped: a send-failure status code with no recipient address, and an error message with no stack trace or URL «a stack could carry an enrolment token».

00:21:41.375 --> 00:21:47.000
Everything else in the log is the hosting platform's, not ours.

00:21:47.762 --> 00:21:50.805
For lolly-tools, hosting is Vercel and

00:21:50.877 --> 00:21:54.140
access-log retention follows Vercel's own

00:21:54.203 --> 00:21:57.268
platform defaults for our plan.

00:21:58.045 --> 00:22:01.156
We configure no log drain, no long-term

00:22:01.216 --> 00:22:04.466
log export, and no analytics or monitoring

00:22:04.527 --> 00:22:06.245
product on top.

00:22:06.998 --> 00:22:10.124
We keep no copy of these logs ourselves,

00:22:10.179 --> 00:22:13.030
which also means we have no way to search

00:22:13.089 --> 00:22:15.620
them for you - see Your rights.

00:22:16.764 --> 00:22:20.294
Legal bases, retention and recipients

00:22:21.209 --> 00:22:24.525
Almost nothing here needs a legal basis,

00:22:24.585 --> 00:22:27.569
because almost nothing is processed.

00:22:28.343 --> 00:22:31.494
For completeness, the entire list:

00:22:31.875 --> 00:22:33.950
Recipients.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:40.550
The categories of recipient are: our hosting provider «Vercel Inc.»

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:49.150
, and - only if you use the email sign-in option - a transactional email provider «Resend».

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:59.675
If you sign in with GitHub, Google or SUSE Okta, you interact with that provider directly under their own privacy policy.

00:23:00.025 --> 00:23:05.225
They tell us a verified email address and nothing else.

00:23:05.575 --> 00:23:14.450
We share personal data with no one else, and we do not sell data, run advertising, or profile users.

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:18.200
Transfers outside the EEA.

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:22.950
Vercel and Resend are US companies.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:39.850
Function compute for lolly-tools is pinned to Vercel's Frankfurt «fra1» region so processing happens in the EU, but as US-headquartered providers they may still access data as processors from the US.

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:55.400
Those transfers rely on the European Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses and/or the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, as set out in each provider's data processing agreement.

00:23:55.750 --> 00:24:10.600
Because the personal data reaching either provider is so limited - an email address passed through to send one message, and ordinary access logs - the exposure is correspondingly small.

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:14.425
Automated decision-making.

00:24:14.775 --> 00:24:16.250
None.

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:25.750
There is no profiling and no automated decision producing legal or similarly significant effects «Art.

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:28.200
22».

00:24:29.290 --> 00:24:31.295
Children's privacy

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:43.775
Lolly does not knowingly collect personal information from anyone, of any age, in the ordinary course of using the app - there is nothing to collect.

00:24:44.125 --> 00:24:58.375
The one place personal information «an email address» is ever gathered is Content Credentials enrolment, described above, which is not directed at or intended for children.

00:24:59.499 --> 00:25:00.718
Your rights

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:26.475
Because almost everything Lolly touches is stored only on your own device, most of what data-protection law calls "your rights" - access, correction, deletion, portability - are things you can already do yourself, instantly, without asking anyone: your data lives in your browser's storage, in a form you can inspect, export «Export my data & render everything, above», or delete «Profile → Clear all

00:25:26.825 --> 00:25:29.025
my data».

00:25:29.375 --> 00:25:57.250
Formally, under GDPR Articles 15 to 22 you have the right to access your personal data, to rectify it, to erase it, to restrict or object to its processing «including objecting to anything we base on legitimate interests», to data portability, and - where processing rests on consent - to withdraw that consent at any time, without affecting the lawfulness of what happened before you withdrew it.

00:25:57.999 --> 00:26:01.226
Here is the honest position on exercising

00:26:01.289 --> 00:26:03.243
them against us.

00:26:04.019 --> 00:26:07.084
Since we no longer keep an issuance log,

00:26:07.143 --> 00:26:10.132
we hold no personal data about you that we

00:26:10.192 --> 00:26:13.645
can look up, correct, export or delete.

00:26:14.393 --> 00:26:17.140
If you write and ask what we have on you,

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:19.768
the truthful answer is nothing, and we

00:26:19.826 --> 00:26:21.370
will say so.

00:26:22.147 --> 00:26:25.306
The one category that exists at all is

00:26:25.368 --> 00:26:28.508
hosting access logs keyed to an IP

00:26:28.569 --> 00:26:31.756
address, held by our hosting provider

00:26:31.832 --> 00:26:34.419
under their retention defaults.

00:26:35.185 --> 00:26:37.655
We have no facility to search or

00:26:37.747 --> 00:26:40.980
selectively delete those, and we will tell

00:26:41.069 --> 00:26:44.219
you that rather than pretend otherwise.

00:26:44.983 --> 00:26:47.685
Everything that is actually yours is on

00:26:47.747 --> 00:26:51.012
your device, where you can already read,

00:26:51.082 --> 00:26:53.704
export and destroy it without asking

00:26:53.786 --> 00:26:55.569
anyone's permission.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:59.400
You have the right to complain.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:18.375
If you think we have handled your data improperly, you can lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory authority - in the EU, the authority in your country of residence, place of work, or where you believe the infringement occurred «Art.

00:27:18.725 --> 00:27:21.075
77».

00:27:21.425 --> 00:27:31.300
Our lead supervisory authority is the Bayerisches Landesamt für Datenschutzaufsicht «BayLDA» in Ansbach, Germany.

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:38.175
You do not need to contact us first, though we would like the chance to fix it.

00:27:38.937 --> 00:27:41.468
We don't sell data.

00:27:42.253 --> 00:27:45.068
We don't have any to sell.

00:27:46.179 --> 00:27:48.733
Changes to this policy

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:54.350
The date at the top changes whenever this document does.

00:27:54.700 --> 00:28:09.600
A change that alters what leaves your device or what's retained gets its own line here, not a silent edit - if you want to see what changed, ask «below» or compare against the public source.

00:28:10.695 --> 00:28:14.313
